  Advantages / Disadvantages                                                                                              
                                                                                                                          
  Alpa: Compile + run each (stage, submesh) candidate                                                                     
                                                                                                                          
  Advantages:     
  - Accurate costs — measures real latency including kernel fusion, memory layout effects, CUDA overhead. The formula
  bytes/bandwidth is only an approximation; real execution captures everything.                                           
  - No global topology assumption — works for any cluster shape (3 GPUs, 5 GPUs, asymmetric topology). No power-of-2
  constraint.                                                                                                             
  - Captures non-linearity — communication cost isn't perfectly linear in bytes. Small tensors hit latency-dominated      
  regime, large tensors hit bandwidth-dominated regime. Real profiling captures this automatically.                 
  - Adapts to heterogeneous stages — each stage is profiled independently with its own submesh, so a heavy stage on a big 
  submesh and a light stage on a small submesh are both measured correctly.
                                                                                                                          
  Disadvantages:  
  - Enormous search cost — Algorithm 1 shows it must compile and run every (stage_i..stage_j, submesh_shape) combination. 
  For K layers and S submesh shapes, that's O(K²·S) actual GPU executions before training starts. For large models this   
  can take hours.                                                                                                      
  - Requires JAX/XLA — Alpa's profiling is tied to XLA's ahead-of-time compilation. The "compile then profile" loop only  
  works because XLA can produce a standalone executable for a subgraph. PyTorch's eager mode can't do this as cleanly.  
  - Memory pressure during search — running actual GPU kernels during planning consumes device memory, limits what can be 
  explored simultaneously.                                                                                               
  - Cold start — no benefit from caching without explicit infrastructure. Every new model configuration needs a full      
  re-search.      
                                                                                                                          
  ---             
  ColossalAI: Upfront α/β benchmark → analytical cost model                                                               
                  
  Advantages:
  - Fast planning — profile_ab() runs once (~seconds for a cluster), then all cost estimates are computed analytically. No
   GPU executions during search.                                                                                          
  - Framework-agnostic — α/β is just numbers; the cost model is pure math. Works in PyTorch eager mode without any
  compiler infrastructure.                                                                                                
  - Handles non-uniform topology — measuring actual pair-wise α/β captures partial NVLink coverage (e.g. NVLink only      
  between GPU 0-3, PCIe for GPU 4-7). Alpa can't distinguish this because it just takes NVLink/InfiniBand as given  
  constants.                                                                                                              
  - Reusable across models — cluster α/β doesn't change between models. Profile the cluster once, plan any model.
  - Simpler implementation — the intra-op ILP uses a formula, not a subprocess that compiles and runs JAX executables.    
                                                                                                                          
  Disadvantages:                                                                                                          
  - Less accurate costs — α + β × bytes is a linear model. It misses kernel fusion savings, memory layout effects, CUDA   
  graph optimizations, etc.                                                                                               
  - Power-of-2 constraint — search_best_logical_mesh() requires world_size to be a power-of-2 for the balanced 2D grid.
  - Formula misses op-level detail — two different operators with the same byte count get the same communication cost     
  estimate, even if their actual collective latency differs (e.g. all-reduce vs all-to-all behave differently on real     
  hardware).                                                                                                              
  - Single global mesh — the whole cluster is forced into one balanced grid before the search starts. You can't have stage
   0 on a 1×2 mesh and stage 1 on a 1×4 mesh with different topological arrangement.                                      
                                                                                                                          
