  Why did ColossalAI choose the "simpler" approach?
                                                                                                                          
  This is the most interesting question. Several concrete reasons:
                                                                                                                          
  1. Framework target: PyTorch, not JAX                                                                                   
   
  Alpa's "compile then profile" approach works because JAX/XLA can compile a subgraph into a standalone executable and run
   it in isolation. PyTorch in 2022-2023 (when ColossalAI was built) was primarily eager-mode. torch.compile (TorchDynamo)
   was still experimental. You can't easily take a subgraph of a PyTorch model, compile it into a standalone binary, and  
  run it on a submesh of GPUs without the full training context. So Alpa's profiling strategy was not portable to PyTorch
  at the time.

  2. Planning speed was the explicit design goal

  The ColossalAI paper explicitly says their symbolic profiler avoids "time-consuming" ahead-of-time profiling. They      
  prioritized making the planner fast — one α/β benchmark run + pure math = planning in seconds. Alpa's hours-long search
  was a known complaint in the community.                                                                                 
                  
  3. Influenced by Alpa but solving a different problem                                                                   
   
  ColossalAI adopted Alpa's intra-op ILP solver (the paper says: "Our solver is adapted from Alpa's intra-op parallel ILP 
  solver") but replaced Alpa's inter-op DP with real profiling with a faster analytical version. They took the part of
  Alpa that worked in PyTorch (the ILP formulation) and replaced the part that didn't (compile + run per submesh).        
                  
  4. The power-of-2 constraint wasn't a problem for their target use case                                                 
   
  The original 3D parallel design — Megatron-LM style — always uses pp × tp × dp where all three are powers-of-2. 8 GPUs, 
  16 GPUs, 32 GPUs. For that use case, the constraint never triggers. The power-of-2 requirement is only a problem when
  you try to use non-standard GPU counts like our Phase 3 (3 GPUs), which is a newer idea that the original design didn't 
  anticipate.     

  5. "Optimal" is relative to the tradeoff you're optimizing                                                              
   
  Alpa's approach is more accurate but costs hours of planning. ColossalAI's approach is less accurate but costs seconds. 
  If the accuracy difference only affects the final throughput by a few percent, the ColossalAI approach is actually
  better in practice — you don't lose hours to planning on every new model or configuration. "Less optimal" in cost model 
  accuracy can be "more optimal" in total time-to-results.

  ---
  The core tradeoff in one sentence
                                   
  ▎ Alpa trades planning time for plan accuracy (correct for any topology, JAX/XLA-only). ColossalAI trades plan accuracy 
  ▎ for planning speed and PyTorch compatibility, but inherits the rectangular-grid assumption that breaks for            
  ▎ non-power-of-2 world sizes.
                                                                                                                          
  Our Phase 3 work sits right at that crack: we need variable stage sizes (which Alpa handles naturally) but we're in     
  PyTorch (where Alpa's compile+profile loop doesn't work), so we patched around the power-of-2 constraint analytically
  rather than doing full per-submesh profiling.